WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.680
Good Morning, and welcome back to another episode&nbsp;
of The Joy of Mathematics. I'm Toby and today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:11.720
we're going to be talking about the world of white&nbsp;
holes. You might have heard of black holes before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:17.080
these scary sinks of gravitational potential&nbsp;
from which nothing can escape. Black holes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:22.200
were predicted to exist a long time ago and&nbsp;
then scientists eventually found evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:27.720
that they do exist. Well, white holes are&nbsp;
kind of like the opposite of black holes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:33.920
they are born in the depths of a black hole&nbsp;
where time dances backwards. Nothing can enter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:39.840
a white hole but everything inside of it will&nbsp;
eventually be pushed out. For now white holes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:45.200
remain just a mathematical prediction and we&nbsp;
haven't detected any but thinking about white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:50.840
holes helps us to understand the physics in a&nbsp;
deeper way. This video was inspired by Carlo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:56.160
Rovelli's latest book White Holes and thank&nbsp;
you to Riverhead Books for sending me a copy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.240
The book doesn't try to convince you that&nbsp;
white holes exist because they might not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:08.080
instead it talks about what it is like to play in&nbsp;
the mathematical sandpit of these abstract ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.160
abstract as a white hole and what it means&nbsp;
to think about them and to research them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:17.800
Rovelli's book will be available wherever&nbsp;
books are sold in the US from the 31st&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:23.880
of October and that book will help you to&nbsp;
understand these concepts in a deeper way.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:29.200
Black holes are as abundant as the stars that&nbsp;
twinkle in the night sky. In fact many black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:35.240
holes are formed from dying Stars. When stars&nbsp;
are alive they are radiant burning hydrogen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.720
"Every day is a good day when you're a star"&nbsp;
you could imagine them singing for billions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:45.560
harmonious years. But eventually they will have&nbsp;
a bad day. Their fuel is steadily running out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:51.400
turning into helium and other elements. When&nbsp;
the most enormous stars run out of fuel they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:57.800
collapse under the irresistible pool of gravity&nbsp;
becoming black holes. Matter isn't swallowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:04.600
into oblivion instead it's spirals inward towards&nbsp;
the central star within the black hole. There it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:10.200
continues to exist even though light from&nbsp;
this matter cannot escape to the outside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:18.880
it is enveloped in mystery held&nbsp;
prisoner by a gravitational pull.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:25.040
Black holes are often portrayed like this it's&nbsp;
what looks like 2D flat space warping into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.560
funnel but this isn't the whole picture. This&nbsp;
common drawing is not a mistake but a happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:36.840
accident born from the need for simplicity. Given&nbsp;
that stars are spherical their collapsed version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:44.440
is also spherical. The Event Horizon, the point&nbsp;
of no return, is not a circle but instead is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:50.560
sphere. If we were inside a black hole we would&nbsp;
find ourselves trapped in ever smaller spheres&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:56.480
as we sunk deeper into its depths. Drawings&nbsp;
like this are missing a dimension, to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:02.840
more accurate every circle should actually be&nbsp;
a sphere. The spheres would be overlapping on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:08.720
top of each other like a Russian doll. Drawings&nbsp;
like this are more of a map of gravity's well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:14.120
From the center it would require an enormous&nbsp;
amount of energy to climb out and since black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:28.040
holes are symmetrical in all directions this&nbsp;
slice is okay to represent the entire sphere.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:33.800
We can paint a horizon with a a single brush&nbsp;
stroke it signifies the boundary of our sight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:40.880
separating water from sky. A ship sailing over&nbsp;
the horizon won't stop existing once it passes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:46.800
it and the sailors wouldn't notice it's just us&nbsp;
onlookers back on the beach who will think the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:52.520
boat has disappeared. The same is true for the&nbsp;
horizon of a black hole. If our friend Carlo was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:58.240
to travel to a large black hole and managed&nbsp;
to cross the Event Horizon he wouldn't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:03.280
notice. Super-massive black holes are large&nbsp;
enough that changes in gravity are spread out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:07.880
and they are less likely to hurt you. It's only&nbsp;
the small black holes you need to worry about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:12.560
they could turn you into spaghetti because&nbsp;
gravity's well is very steep. Carlo's watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.880
continues ticking at its normal rate but to&nbsp;
us watching from the outside he would slow to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:23.480
a stop. Past the Horizon Carlo would disappear&nbsp;
from our sight because the light from him could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:29.160
no longer reach us but Carlo himself would be&nbsp;
happily bobbing along deeper into the black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:34.480
hole. Many textbooks condemn whoever enters&nbsp;
a black hole but don't worry Carlo could come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:45.040
out again he just needs to find another way&nbsp;
out and that happens to be via a white hole.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:51.280
Einstein gifted us the idea that gravity is the&nbsp;
distortion of spacetime where the spacetime grid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:57.760
represents events each with a position and&nbsp;
a time. So the gravity well of a black hole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:04.200
can be represented by a funnel of space time&nbsp;
and as the black hole ages the shape changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:09.160
lengthening and growing narrower with the&nbsp;
passage of time. We only know this thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:14.920
Einstein's equations which illuminate that which&nbsp;
would otherwise be invisible to us. Einstein's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:20.920
equations do have a limitation though they can't&nbsp;
be used to describe extreme conditions and for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:27.080
the black hole things do get extreme. As the&nbsp;
diameter of the funnel narrows the cylinder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:33.080
becomes more curved like a roll that is rolled&nbsp;
tighter and tighter. This distortion of spacetime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:42.440
is so severe that Einstein's equations no longer&nbsp;
work, luckily though quantum physics can save us.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:47.200
When it rains and water falls from the&nbsp;
sky it doesn't fall in one continuous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:52.720
flow instead it falls in millions of&nbsp;
tiny individual droplets. Each happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:57.840
little droplet could be called a quanta&nbsp;
of rain where the word quanta means the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:03.240
smallest unit that makes up a whole. Now&nbsp;
let your imagination wander to light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:08.720
it too can be thought of as a shower of&nbsp;
light drops instead of a continuous flow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:14.760
These individual bundles or quanta of light are&nbsp;
what we are describing with quantum physics. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:21.760
could also think about splitting space itself up&nbsp;
into quanta. If each little piece of SpaceTime is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:26.960
like a grain of sand then together they make&nbsp;
up the beach of our universe. The inside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:33.880
a black hole cannot be squashed any further than&nbsp;
the size of the individual grains of spacetime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:45.160
this is where Einstein's equations stop working,&nbsp;
here space is distorted on the quantum level.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:52.400
If I drop a ball it falls to the ground then&nbsp;
bounces back up. In a black hole spacetime is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:59.480
falling upon itself so shouldn't it also be able&nbsp;
to bounce back? When a ball bounces it's as if a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:06.200
film of its fall were being played in reverse.&nbsp;
If we played a film of a black hole in reverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:11.520
what would we see we would see? The&nbsp;
star and spacetime bouncing back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:18.240
we would see a white hole. A white hole is a&nbsp;
black hole played backwards in time. Quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:24.760
physics links the two spacetimes because the&nbsp;
transition occurs when space is squashed beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:30.640
its granular limit. In quantum physics things&nbsp;
don't always have a position they can be nowhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:38.120
living in a haze of probabilities. This fact&nbsp;
allows particles to leap or tunnel through space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:50.640
and grains of space themselves might also be able&nbsp;
to leap, flipping a black hole to a white one.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:57.040
In a black hole you can enter but not leave and&nbsp;
in a white hole you can leave but not enter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:03.240
Anything that enters a black hole can dance with&nbsp;
the limits of space and time before passing into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:09.600
a white hole and coming out again. An unsettling&nbsp;
thought is that from the outside we can't easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:14.480
distinguish a black hole from a white one.&nbsp;
One may expect that with the hands of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:20.360
running backwards that the pull of gravity would&nbsp;
turn into a push but that's not the case. In a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:26.120
film of the solar system being run backwards&nbsp;
the sun still pulls the planets in a cosmic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:32.640
embrace. Nothing can cross the Horizon and enter&nbsp;
a white hole but watching from afar we never see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:38.240
anything cross the horizon and enter a black&nbsp;
hole either. To us Carlo's time would appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:44.000
to slow as he approached the Horizon of a black&nbsp;
hole crawling to a stop. It would seem to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:55.200
an Infinity for him to cross the horizon and&nbsp;
even if we stayed up late we'd never catch it.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:00.600
With black and white holes appearing the same&nbsp;
from afar we are reminded of the importance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:06.480
of looking beyond what can be immediately seen.&nbsp;
We might not be correct about white holes. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.240
might not exist at all or have properties&nbsp;
that we weren't anticipating. One idea is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:17.880
that a black hole and a white hole could be&nbsp;
linked forming a kind of wormhole between two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:24.040
universes or two regions of space bridging&nbsp;
across the fourth dimension. The scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:29.880
who have given us these ideas have done their&nbsp;
best to paint accurate pictures but paintings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:35.720
require a bit of creativity and in that way&nbsp;
scientists are artists. I think there's an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:41.880
artist hidden at the heart of every one of us.&nbsp;
You too can paint inspiring pictures so how would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:47.920
you imagine the inside of a white hole? Having&nbsp;
doubts is okay, that's the thrill of science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:56.360
always wondering if what you've done is correct&nbsp;
with uncertainty growing into new bigger ideas.

00:09:56.360 --> 00:10:02.240
Carlo Rovelli is a physicist who has spent much&nbsp;
of his life studying the quantum aspects of space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:09.480
and time and his book White Holes is not just&nbsp;
about explaining the scientific ideas but is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:15.640
about the mind of a scientist at work. He mentions&nbsp;
other ideas of white holes including that the Big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:22.280
Bang which created this universe of ours could&nbsp;
have been a white hole that was formed from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:28.560
collapsing universe bouncing back. Rovelli writes&nbsp;
in a very poetic way that can communicate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:34.520
ideas of physics to someone who doesn't know much&nbsp;
about it but also can offer new perspectives for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:39.760
someone who knows a lot already. There's a link to&nbsp;
order a copy of his book down in the description.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:42.760
Thank you to Riverhead books for&nbsp;
sponsoring this video and thank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:49.760
you for watching. I hope that you&nbsp;
have an absolutely mathematical day!

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:52.880
Thanks for watching this video and&nbsp;
thanks to my Patreon supporters for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:59.040
making it possible a special shout out&nbsp;
